
-MAC addressing: 
The MAC stands for Media Access Control. The MAC address and the IP address are two different things. We already discussed the IP addressing in detail above.
We just talked about the MAC address now.
This is a unique 12-digit hexadecimal address (separated by colon or hyphen) assigned by an organisation at the time of manufacturing. This is the non-changeable physical address of a device in a network. This is very important when we want to communicate with devices in a LAN network.
The MAC address works at the 2nd layer of the OSI model to send and receive data between devices. The first 6 digits give us the information about the manufacturer, and the last 6 digits for the network device. 
Examples: 
00:1A:2B – Apple Inc.
3C:5A: B4 – Cisco Systems
AC:67: B2 – Intel Corporation
The full MAC address looks like:
00:1A:2B:3C: 4D:5E (colon-separated)
00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E (hyphen-separated)

Type of MAC addresses:
There are 3 main types of MAC addresses:
1. unicast MAC address: When we want a connection between just a server and a device, we use the unicast MAC address. This build connection is actually between two devices in a network.
2. Multicast MAC address: When we want to pass a message between a small group of devices at a single time, we use this address. This starts from 01:00:5 and is used normally for streaming, video calling, and gaming. 
3. Broadcast MAC Address (similar to Broadcast Address): The MAC address FF: FF:FF:FF:FF:FF is used for sending requests or data segments to each device present in a network at the same time. This is similar to broadcast IP addresses in a network, but this is used only for a local network.
Can it be changed?
Yes, the MAC address can be changeable, but just at the software level. We cannot change the real MAC address assigned by a manufacturer. Some particular software puts a layer of a random MAC address on the original one. Like in Kali Linux, we use the built-in feature (MAC changer) to change our MAC address temporarily at the software level.
